====================================================================================================================================================================
======================================================   Script to Check disk space.     ===========================================================================
====================================================================================================================================================================


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
You can monitor disk utilization using the df command. To automate this, you can write a Bash script that extracts the usage percentage of your mounted filesystems and triggers an alert if any exceed a specified threshold.

Here is a script that checks all local filesystems and warns you if any pass a defined limit:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#!/bin/bash

# Set the maximum allowed disk usage percentage
THRESHOLD=80

# Run df for local filesystems, skip the header row, and process usage/mount points
df -hl | awk 'NR>1 {print $5, $6}' | sed 's/%//g' | while read USAGE MOUNT; do
    
    # Ensure USAGE is a number (ignores empty or non-standard lines)
    if [[ "$USAGE" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        if [ "$USAGE" -ge "$THRESHOLD" ]; then
            echo "WARNING: Disk utilization on $MOUNT is at ${USAGE}% (Threshold: ${THRESHOLD}%)"
            
            # Note: You can replace the echo above with the mail command from the previous script
            # echo "Disk $MOUNT is at ${USAGE}%" | mail -s "Disk Alert: $MOUNT" admin@example.com
        fi
    fi
done


---------------------
How to use it:
---------------------

1. Save the code to a file, for example: check_disk.sh

2. Make the script executable:

chmod +x check_disk.sh

3. Run the script:

./check_disk.sh


====================================================================================================================================================================
=========================================================   Script to send alerts over email.     ==================================================================
====================================================================================================================================================================

Mail alert in bin/bash

Bash relies on command-line mail utilities like mail, mailx, or sendmail to send email notifications, as it has no built-in SMTP client. The most common approach is piping text into the mail command.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Prerequisites

Your system must have a mail utility installed and an underlying Mail Transfer Agent (MTA) like Postfix or Sendmail configured.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For Debian/Ubuntu:

sudo apt install mailutils

For RHEL/CentOS:

sudo yum install mailx

--------------------------------------
The Standard Bash Script Pattern

You can pipe a string directly into the mail command. Here is a robust script template using variables for clean formatting:
--------------------------------------

#!/bin/bash

# Configuration
RECIPIENT="admin@example.com"
SUBJECT="Server Alert: High CPU Usage"
BODY="Warning: CPU usage has exceeded 90% on $(hostname) at $(date)."

# Send the email
echo "$BODY" | mail -s "$SUBJECT" "$RECIPIENT"

# Check if the command succeeded
if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "Notification sent successfully."
else
    echo "Failed to send notification."
fi

--------------------------------------
Advanced Use Cases
-----------------------------------------------------------
Sending a file's contents as the body:
Instead of echo, use input redirection to send a log file:
-----------------------------------------------------------


mail -s "Daily Log Report" admin@example.com < /var/log/daily_report.log

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sending attachments:
If your mail version supports the -A or -a flag (depends on the OS), you can attach files directly. Alternatively, mutt is highly reliable for attachments:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Using mail (Ubuntu/Debian)
echo "See attached backup." | mail -s "Database Backup" -A /backups/db.sql admin@example.com

# Using mutt (Universal)
echo "See attached backup." | mutt -s "Database Backup" -a /backups/db.sql -- admin@example.com

====================================================================================================================================================================
===================================================   Script to Check application is running or not     ============================================================
====================================================================================================================================================================

Creating a reliable bash script to monitor application status requires checking active process IDs (PIDs) or systemd service states. The most robust tool for standard processes is pgrep, as it avoids the common pitfalls of the older ps aux | grep method (which often accidentally matches the grep command itself).

Here is the Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) for scripting an application status check.

Determine the Check Method
Before writing the script, identify how the application runs on the system:

System Services (systemd): If the application runs as a background service (e.g., Nginx, Apache, MySQL), use systemctl is-active.

Standalone Processes: If the application is an independent binary or script, use pgrep -x to search for the exact process name.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Construct the Script
Create a new file named check_app.sh and populate it with the appropriate logic.

Option A: For Standalone Processes (Using pgrep)
-------------------------------------------------

#!/bin/bash

# Define the exact name of the process
APP_NAME="python3"

# pgrep -x looks for an exact match. 
# > /dev/null suppresses the PID output so the script stays clean.
if pgrep -x "$APP_NAME" > /dev/null; then
    echo "[OK] $APP_NAME is currently running."
else
    echo "[WARNING] $APP_NAME is NOT running."
    # Actionable step: You can uncomment the line below to restart it
    # /path/to/start_app.sh &
fi

Option B: For Systemd Services (Using systemctl)
-------------------------------------------------

#!/bin/bash

SERVICE_NAME="nginx"

# is-active returns 0 (true) if running, non-zero otherwise
if systemctl is-active --quiet "$SERVICE_NAME"; then
    echo "[OK] Service $SERVICE_NAME is active."
else
    echo "[CRITICAL] Service $SERVICE_NAME is down."
    # Actionable step: Restart the service
    # sudo systemctl restart "$SERVICE_NAME"
fi

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Execution and Verification

1. Assign Execute Permissions:

chmod +x check_app.sh

2. Test the Positive State: Start your application, run ./check_app.sh, and verify it outputs the [OK] message.

3. Test the Negative State: Stop your application, run ./check_app.sh, and verify it outputs the [WARNING] or [CRITICAL] message.


============================================================================================================================================
=================================   Script for Backup / Removal of logs file after 5-7 days    =============================================
============================================================================================================================================

The most reliable way to manage log retention in a bash script is using the find command combined with the -mtime (modified time) flag.

Test Before Deleting
Always run your find command without the -delete or -exec rm flag first (e.g., find /your/log/dir -type f -name "*.log" -mtime +7) to verify exactly which files will be affected before running the destructive script.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Option 1: Script to Remove Old Logs
If you simply want to purge log files older than 7 days to free up disk space, use this script.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#!/bin/bash

# Configuration
LOG_DIR="/var/log/myapp"
DAYS_TO_KEEP=7

# Find and delete .log files older than 7 days
find "$LOG_DIR" -type f -name "*.log" -mtime +"$DAYS_TO_KEEP" -delete

# Verification step
if [ $? -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "[OK] Logs older than $DAYS_TO_KEEP days have been removed."
else
    echo "[ERROR] Failed to clean up logs in $LOG_DIR."
fi

------------- Note: The + in +"$DAYS_TO_KEEP" is critical. It tells find to match files modified strictly greater than 7 days ago. -------------

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Option 2: Script to Backup and Compress Old Logs
If compliance or debugging requires you to keep the logs, this script moves files older than 5 days into an archive directory and compresses them into a single tar.gz file before removing the originals.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


#!/bin/bash

# Configuration
LOG_DIR="/var/log/myapp"
BACKUP_DIR="/archive/myapp_logs"
DAYS_TO_KEEP=5
TIMESTAMP=$(date +"%Y%m%d_%H%M%S")
ARCHIVE_NAME="logs_backup_$TIMESTAMP.tar.gz"

# Ensure the backup directory exists
mkdir -p "$BACKUP_DIR"

# Step 1: Compress files older than 5 days directly into the backup folder
# xargs is used here to safely handle a large number of files or spaces in filenames
find "$LOG_DIR" -type f -name "*.log" -mtime +"$DAYS_TO_KEEP" -print0 | xargs -0 tar -czf "$BACKUP_DIR/$ARCHIVE_NAME"

# Step 2: If the backup succeeded, delete the original files
if [ -f "$BACKUP_DIR/$ARCHIVE_NAME" ]; then
    find "$LOG_DIR" -type f -name "*.log" -mtime +"$DAYS_TO_KEEP" -delete
    echo "[OK] Old logs backed up to $ARCHIVE_NAME and removed from source."
else
    echo "[ERROR] Backup failed. Original logs were not deleted."
fi


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Deployment
Save your chosen script (e.g., log_cleanup.sh), make it executable using chmod +x log_cleanup.sh, and schedule it in cron to run automatically (e.g., daily at midnight: 0 0 * * * /path/to/log_cleanup.sh).
--------------------------------------------------------------------------




